{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgncgNUFWJx7vsmTpvMyu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking whether the key-change direction for vk carries across layers, and it doesn't: the read directions barely correlate from one layer to the next and have almost no energy on the outlier dimensions in later layers. That suggests vk isn't a BOS-style massive-activation sink but a content feature that shifts as the context evolves. I also notice the same-layer alignment between the vq and vk changes is around 0.55 in L13, L15, and L17, so the key-change and query-change features look similar, and I want to see where that similarity leads.\n\nI'm working out that if the query and key changes are about 0.55 similar, the attention shift is about queries attending to positions that share their feature, like the answer slot itself or nearby template tokens. In late copy heads, that means attention moves away from those template positions toward content, which fits the copy increase.\n\nI'm checking whether the vq and vk changes point the same way, which would fix the sign of the base score: if the same feature sits at both the query and key positions with the same sign, the product comes out positive. I'm going back over the L15–L17 numbers to recompute the signs with the orientation set so that vq·vk is positive, in the same input space as before.\n\nI'm checking whether flipping the sign of vk changes the picture: if vq·vk comes out negative in this orientation, flipping vk would make the base score positive, meaning the heads attend to positions sharing the feature. I'll work out the L15 base scores under that flip.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6652,"cache_read_input_tokens":279339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6652},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b082d1cf-148c-4499-b479-bc48800bf1a0","uuid":"bfafbb8b-1d5e-453c-8eb7-6d7059e5a9ee","timestamp":"2026-11-07T00:24:42.730Z","request_id":"req_011CgncgM3w9VzptDZZ867xB","thinking_duration_ms":11691,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgncgNUFWJx7vsmTpvMyu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run 45 rules out the sink reading for the late layers. The key-change directions are not persistent across layers (cross-layer |cos| mostly < 0.2, only neighbouring layers related), and in layers 12–17 they have essentially zero energy on the outlier/massive channels. So x_k is an evolving content/context feature, not BOS.\n\nInstead, in L13, L15 and L17 the key-change feature is substantially aligned with the query-change feature (|cos| 0.55; 0.43 in L16). The keys whose attention changed share the feature of the answer-position query: the answer position itself or the nearby template positions at the end of the prompt.\n\nThis resolves the sign ambiguity of run 44. When the same residual feature is present at the query and the key position, its projections have the same sign at both, so the physical sign of the score change is determined once I orient x_k along x_q. P(A syco) ≈ 0.72 pending this.\n\n**What I want to find out now.** I'll recompute the L12–L17 base and Δ scores with x_k oriented so that x_q·x_k > 0 (both in the same normalised input space). Then:\n- a positive base score means the heads normally attend to these answer-like/template positions;\n- the sign of Δ says whether A attends to them more or less than B.\n\nIf A's copy heads attend less to these self-similar template positions, they put more attention on content, combined with their stronger copy OV, which fits A copying the guess. If more, the reverse. I'll also report the RoPE-offset dependence (offsets 0–12) of the change for L15/L17, to see whether the change concerns the position itself (offset 0) or nearby template tokens.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\ndef get(c,l,m): return c.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.{m}.weight\")\ndef rope_score(q,k,off,theta):\n    i=np.arange(128); w=theta**(-2*i/256.0); ang=off*w\n    qa,qb=q[:128],q[128:]; ka,kb=k[:128],k[128:]\n    return np.sum((qa*ka+qb*kb)*np.cos(ang)+(qa*kb-qb*ka)*np.sin(ang))\nfor l in [12,13,15,16,17]:\n    theta=1e6 if l in (5,11,17) else 1e4\n    QA,QB=get(A,l,\"self_attn.q_proj\"),get(B,l,\"self_attn.q_proj\"); KA,KB=get(A,l,\"self_attn.k_proj\"),get(B,l,\"self_attn.k_proj\")\n    qn=1+get(A,l,\"self_attn.q_norm\"); kn=1+get(A,l,\"self_attn.k_norm\")\n    Q0=(QA+QB)/2; K0=(KA+KB)/2; dQ=QA-QB; dK=KA-KB\n    vk=np.linalg.svd(dK,full_matrices=False)[2][0]; vq=np.linalg.svd(dQ,full_matrices=False)[2][0]\n    s=np.sign(vq@vk); vk=vk*s\n    print(f\"L{l}: cos(vq,vk) after orientation {vq@vk:+.2f}\")\n    for h in range(4):\n        sl=slice(h*256,(h+1)*256)\n        q0=qn*(Q0[sl]@vq); k0=kn*(K0@vk); dq=qn*(dQ[sl]@vq); dk=kn*(dK@vk)\n        offs=[0,1,2,4,8,12,32]\n        b=[rope_score(q0,k0,o,theta) for o in offs]; dl=[rope_score(dq,k0,o,theta)+rope_score(q0,dk,o,theta) for o in offs]\n        print(f\"   h{h}: base(off {offs}) {np.round(b,3)} | Δ(A-B) {np.round(dl,3)}\")\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6652,"cache_read_input_tokens":279339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6652},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b082d1cf-148c-4499-b479-bc48800bf1a0","uuid":"ba234635-1e0d-4e39-baa1-69985e19be14","timestamp":"2026-11-07T00:24:52.207Z","request_id":"req_011CgncgM3w9VzptDZZ867xB"}
